# Cache Call-Relationship Code-Reading Notes

Entry file: `jetcache-core/src/main/java/com/alicp/jetcache/Cache.java`

This record still points to an interface file, not the implementation class of a particular cache backend. After opening the file, look at the package name and type declaration first, then the default methods; trying to infer the call path backward from the implementation class name makes it easy to mix up the entry points provided by the interface with the backend's own processing.

```java
package com.alicp.jetcache;

import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import java.io.Closeable;
```

The file's type declaration is:

```java
public interface Cache<K, V> extends Closeable {
```

`K` and `V` are the interface's two type parameters, while `Closeable` attaches the close capability at the interface level. What can be confirmed here is the declaration relationship. The file header contains `Logger` and `LoggerFactory`, but based on the imports alone, do not expand this into specific logging calls or attribute the close operation to a particular manager or remote client.

When reviewing it, you can move through the following in-file order:

```text
Cache(type, line 18)
  ├─ get(function, line 39)
  ├─ getAll(function, line 60)
  ├─ put(function, line 73)
  ├─ putAll(function, line 84)
  ├─ putIfAbsent(function, line 98)
  └─ remove(function, line 111)
```

This is a source-reading order, not a runtime call graph. line 18 first establishes the `Cache` type; the `get` on line 39 and the `getAll` on line 60 are read entry points; the `put` on line 73, the `putAll` on line 84, and the `putIfAbsent` on line 98 are write-side names; the `remove` on line 111 separately leaves a delete entry point. The similarly named methods should be considered separately, especially since the single-key `put` cannot replace the batch entry `putAll`.

The three most useful local snippets are still:

```java
default V get(K key) throws CacheInvokeException {
default Map<K, V> getAll(Set<? extends K> keys) throws CacheInvokeException {
default void put(K key, V value) {
```

`get` takes a `K key` as its parameter and returns a `V`; `getAll` accepts `Set<? extends K> keys` and returns `Map<K, V>`. Therefore, a batch read does not pass multiple keys as one ordinary `K`, and its return value is not a single `V` either. Both signatures also include `CacheInvokeException` in the declarations of the read methods. The `put` fragment accepts `K key` and `V value`, returns `void`, and its displayed declaration does not include the same exception name. This records the interface surface only; it does not justify inferring a uniform exception policy for the implementation classes.

The method names form a sufficient local index:

- `get`: read entry point for a single key;
- `getAll`: batch read entry point taking a key set as its parameter;
- `put`: write entry point for a single key and value;
- `putAll`: batch write entry point, which must not be read together with `put`;
- `putIfAbsent`: separately retained conditional-write name;
- `remove`: delete entry point.

Here, “single” and “batch” are distinguished within the file solely according to the parameters and method names. The interface fragment does not expand on this page into the cache region, manager registration, annotation context, or concrete remote implementation, nor does it assume that these objects are direct callers of `Cache.java`. To continue tracing a particular backend, proceed from the actual declaration and call sites in the corresponding implementation class.

Earlier, when only the method names were listed, `getAll`, `putAll`, and `putIfAbsent` could easily be grouped together in the search results. After review, the original line numbers were retained, and the parameters, return types, and exception declarations that could be pasted directly were restored to the body. This way, when entering through the interface, you can first determine whether you are reading a single value, reading a collection, writing a single value, or checking a conditional write, rather than treating similarly named methods as the same path.

The close relationship can be traced from `extends Closeable` to the interface level. The logging relationship can be traced from the two imports in the file header to the class name. If you later need to see how a specific resource is closed, or where the log object is used in a default method, return to the implementation fragment of this file and continue locating it; this page does not extend these two imports into the implementation lifecycle.

The specific relationship with `RedisLettuceCache` is not covered in this `Cache.java` entry record. The local entry point for the related implementation is documented separately in DOC-02; the source boundary on this page stops at the interface declaration, default methods, and file-header dependencies.

